<!--
  aihu-ascii-hero — presentational styled recipe (performativeUI port,
  Slice 10 — docs/plans/2026-08-01-performative-ui-port.md §Tier-C batch 2).

  Design and behavior transcribed in spirit from performativeUI (MIT); no
  source text or `pui-` class names vendored — see NOTICES.md. The upstream
  demo file was NOT read for this port: the effect below is written from the
  behavioral description in the slice brief plus the composable's own API.

  A hero-section text-field effect: a monospaced grid of glyphs shimmering
  behind (or in front of) hero copy, built directly on `@aihu/use/motion`'s
  `useCharacterField` (Track B Slice 8), which is itself built on
  `useCanvasSurface`. Unlike its Slice-10 sibling `node-graph-background`
  — which had to drop a level because `useParticleField` exposes no draw
  seam — this component needs nothing the composable does not already do, so
  it stays at the higher level: the grid, the reflow-on-resize, the three
  animation modes and the reduced-motion at-rest composition are all
  `useCharacterField`'s, and this file only maps attributes onto its options.

  `useCanvasSurface` OWNS canvas creation (see its header comment: measuring
  and styling the same node would feed a `ResizeObserver` a resize loop, so
  it measures the HOST and styles a canvas it creates itself). This
  component's `@template` is therefore a plain container — `ref={hostEl}`
  feeds that host to `useCharacterField`, which mounts its own `<canvas>`
  inside it; nothing here reaches for a `<canvas>` element directly.

  ── Props ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  chars:    the glyph set, as a plain string split per code unit. Default
            "01" — a binary rain reads as "hero for a dev tool", which is
            what this component is for; the composable's own default
            (" .:-=+*#%@", a luminance ramp) is the right default for an
            ASCII-art renderer and the wrong one for a hero backdrop.
  density:  fraction of grid cells that carry a glyph at all, [0, 1],
            string-encoded number. Default "0.6". Deliberately below 1: the
            composable notes that "below 1 the field is sparse, which reads
            as texture rather than a filled block", and a hero background
            that renders as a solid block of characters fights the headline
            sitting on top of it.
  mode:     drift (default) | pulse | reveal — `useCharacterField`'s closed
            mode enum, surfaced rather than hard-coded because all three are
            legitimate hero treatments. `drift` is the default because it is
            the only continuously AMBIENT one: `reveal` is a one-shot
            typewriter wipe that finishes in ~1.5s and then sits static
            forever (right for an entrance, wrong for a persistent
            backdrop), and `pulse` breathes the whole field's opacity in a
            way that pulls the eye off the copy. An unrecognized value falls
            back to `drift` rather than throwing — attributes are untrusted
            strings and a decorative layer must not be able to break a page.
  color:    fill color for the glyphs. Default "#ffffff". This is a LITERAL
            CSS color string, not a `--color-*` token reference — identical
            constraint to `floating-sparkles` (Slice 9) and
            `node-graph-background`: a canvas 2D context's `fillStyle` is not
            part of the CSS cascade and cannot resolve a custom property, so
            it needs an already-resolved value. Exposed (beyond the three
            props the slice brief named) for parity with the rest of the
            Tier-C batch — a decorative canvas layer with no color control is
            unusable on half the surfaces it would be dropped onto.

  Not exposed: `cellSize`, `fontSize`, `fontFamily`, `speed`, `opacity`,
  `revealDuration`. Every one of them is an internal look-and-feel constant
  at this altitude, and the composable's defaults (14px pitch, monospace,
  0.85 alpha) are already the hero-appropriate values. Widening the surface
  ahead of a real need is the over-engineering this port keeps declining.

  ── Layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A standalone, full-bleed, absolutely-positioned decorative layer
  (`position: absolute; inset: 0`), no slot — the same "decorative background
  layer" contract `floating-sparkles`, `aurora` and `node-graph-background`
  carry, for consistency across the Tier-C batch. The consumer places it as
  the first child of a `position: relative` hero section and stacks the real
  headline above it with `z-index` (see `useCanvasSurface`'s header: "the
  host must be positioned" for its owned canvas to land at the host's
  top-left, which `position: absolute` already satisfies).

  ── Reduced motion / off-screen ──────────────────────────────────────────

  Neither is reimplemented here, and — importantly — neither is FOUGHT here:
  no local `prefers-reduced-motion` branch is layered on top, because
  `useCharacterField` already owns a per-mode at-rest composition that a
  naive component-level override would be strictly worse than. Under
  `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useCanvasSurface` paints exactly one
  frame with `reducedMotion: true` and never arms the rAF loop; the field's
  `onFrame` then calls its `atRest()` instead of `step()`, which resets every
  cell to its seeded glyph at full base opacity. That means `drift` freezes
  on its seeded glyphs, `pulse` holds full opacity, and `reveal` shows the
  wipe's END state — every cell already revealed, never a frozen half-wipe.
  A populated field in all three modes, never a blank box.
  `isVisible`-gated pausing while scrolled off-screen is inherited for free.

  `shadow: 'light'` — see the parent execution plan's theming decision.
-->

@state {
  import { cn } from '@aihu/css-engine/runtime/cn'

  shadow: 'light'

  let chars = prop({ default: '01' })
  let density = prop({ default: '0.6' })
  let mode = prop({ default: 'drift' })
  let color = prop({ default: '#ffffff' })

  let hostEl = state(null)

  // Read every prop into a differently-named local before use — an
  // options-object key textually identical to a prop name (`mode: mode`,
  // `density: density`, `color: color`) trips the compiler's prop-auto-unwrap
  // rewriter (see token-stream.aihu's header for the exact failure mode).
  const glyphs = chars.length > 0 ? chars : '01'
  const cellDensity = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, Number(density) || 0))
  const fieldMode = mode === 'pulse' || mode === 'reveal' ? mode : 'drift'
  const inkColor = color

  useCharacterField(() => hostEl, {
    characters: glyphs,
    density: cellDensity,
    mode: fieldMode,
    color: inkColor,
  })
}

@template {
  <div
    ref={hostEl}
    data-slot="ascii-hero"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class={cn('aihu-ascii-hero')}></div>
}

@style {
  .aihu-ascii-hero {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
}
